From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU0 macro mode: VLQI/VSQI/VLQD/VSQD/VILWR/VISWR use VU0 data memory

What broke:
  1. The macro-mode VU memory instructions read and wrote EE RAM at
     vi * 16 (VILWR/VISWR at vi * 4) instead of VU0 data memory (EE
     0x11004000). Ben 10: Protector of Earth uploads bone matrices for its
     software skinning with "vsqi vfN, (vi1++)"; they landed in EE kernel RAM
     at 0x0-0x3FF and the VU0 microprograms (VCALLMS 0x0/0x38/0x140) read
     whatever was in VU0 data memory as matrices.
  2. VSQI/VSQD took the stored register from bits 20-16 and the address
     register from bits 15-11; it is the other way round for stores
     ("VSQI.dest fs, (it++)": fs = bits 15-11, it = bits 20-16;
     0x4be1137d is "vsqi.xyzw vf2, (vi1++)"). The game's four stores of a
     matrix all wrote vf1 to the addresses vi2..vi4.
  3. VILWR/VISWR ignored the field (x/y/z/w) named in the destination mask.

The fix:
  The six instructions address 0x11004000 + (vi & 0xFF) * 16 (VU0 data
  memory is 256 quadwords and wraps; the VU1 register window that VU0 micro
  mode sees at 0x400-0x43F is not modelled), plus 4 * field for
  VILWR/VISWR. VSQI/VSQD read fs from rd and it from rt. VI post-increment
  and pre-decrement wrap at 16 bits (patch 0008 keeps VI0 at 0).
  New ps2xTest case "VU0 macro memory ops address VU0 data memory with the
  right fields".

How it was found:
  findings/18 s.12: a gdb trace of the skinning microprograms showed bone
  "matrices" in VU0 data memory that were vertex positions or zeros; the
  generated code for the VSQI loop at 0x15f358 wrote to EE address vi * 16
  and stored vf1 for "vsqi vf2, (vi1++)".

--- a/ps2xRecomp/src/lib/vu_translation_helpers.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRecomp/src/lib/vu_translation_helpers.cpp
@@ -111,14 +111,42 @@
                            inst.rt, inst.rt);
     }
 
+    // Macro-mode VU memory instructions (VLQI/VSQI/VLQD/VSQD/VILWR/VISWR) address VU0 data
+    // memory, not EE RAM: the address register holds a quadword index into VU0's 4 KB data
+    // memory, seen from the EE at 0x11004000. Only the low 8 bits select a quadword (256
+    // quadwords; VU0 data memory wraps). The VU1 register window that VU0 micro mode sees at
+    // quadwords 0x400-0x43F is not modelled here.
+    namespace
+    {
+        std::string vu0DataQwordAddress(uint8_t vi)
+        {
+            return fmt::format("(0x11004000u + (((uint32_t)ctx->vi[{}] & 0xFFu) << 4))", vi);
+        }
+
+        // VILWR/VISWR name exactly one field (x, y, z or w) in the destination mask.
+        uint32_t vuFieldByteOffset(uint8_t destMask)
+        {
+            if (destMask & 0x8) return 0;  // x
+            if (destMask & 0x4) return 4;  // y
+            if (destMask & 0x2) return 8;  // z
+            return 12;                     // w
+        }
+    }
+
     std::string CodeGenerator::translateVU_VILWR(const Instruction &inst)
     {
-        return fmt::format("{{ uint32_t addr = (uint32_t)(ctx->vi[{}] << 2) & 0x3FFC; ctx->vi[{}] = static_cast<uint16_t>(READ32(addr)); }}", inst.rd, inst.rt); // VILWR.<f> vit, (vis)
+        // VILWR.<f> vit, (vis): vit = low 16 bits of field f of VU0 data quadword vis.
+        if (inst.rt == 0)
+            return "// VILWR to VI0 ignored";
+        return fmt::format("{{ uint32_t addr = {} + {}u; ctx->vi[{}] = static_cast<uint16_t>(READ32(addr)); }}",
+                           vu0DataQwordAddress(inst.rd), vuFieldByteOffset(inst.vectorInfo.vectorField), inst.rt);
     }
 
     std::string CodeGenerator::translateVU_VISWR(const Instruction &inst)
     {
-        return fmt::format("{{ uint32_t addr = (uint32_t)(ctx->vi[{}] << 2) & 0x3FFC; WRITE32(addr, (uint32_t)ctx->vi[{}]); }}", inst.rd, inst.rt); // VISWR.<f> vit, (vis)
+        // VISWR.<f> vit, (vis): field f of VU0 data quadword vis = vit, zero-extended to 32 bits.
+        return fmt::format("{{ uint32_t addr = {} + {}u; WRITE32(addr, (uint32_t)(uint16_t)ctx->vi[{}]); }}",
+                           vu0DataQwordAddress(inst.rd), vuFieldByteOffset(inst.vectorInfo.vectorField), inst.rt);
     }
 
